      "formula": "Kata sifat dari kata kerja (*participial adjectives*) memiliki dua bentuk dengan makna berbeda tajam:\n- **`V-ing` (Active / Cause):** Menjelaskan sesuatu/seseorang yang **MENYEBABKAN** perasaan atau efek (*an **interesting** lecture*, *a **confusing** diagram*, *a **fascinating** discovery*, *an **exhausting** schedule*).\n- **`V3 / -ed` (Passive / Receiver):** Menjelaskan makhluk hidup (biasanya orang) yang **MERASAKAN / MENGALAMI** perasaan tersebut (*an **interested** audience*, ***confused** students*, *a **fascinated** observer*, ***exhausted** engineers*).\n\n**Classic EPrT Trap:** Menukar `-ing` dan `-ed` pada benda mati (`a very bored documentary` -> dokumenter tidak bisa merasa bosan; harus `boring`!) atau pada perasaan manusia (`The researchers were exciting about the grant` -> harus `excited`!).\n\n**WRONG:** Although the cryptography lecture was highly technical, the undergraduate students remained **fascinating** throughout the session.\n**RIGHT:** Although the cryptography lecture was highly technical, the undergraduate students remained **fascinated** throughout the session.\n\n**WRONG:** Several commuters complained that the revised transit timetable was unnecessarily **confused**.\n**RIGHT:** Several commuters complained that the revised transit timetable was unnecessarily **confusing**.\n\n**Flashcard Q:** When do you use \"surprising\" vs \"surprised\", and \"exhausting\" vs \"exhausted\"?\n**Flashcard A:** Use -ing for the CAUSE of the feeling (a surprising result, an exhausting task); use -ed for the RECEIVER experiencing the feeling (a surprised researcher, an exhausted athlete).\n\n---",
