      "formula": "Written Expression sering menyelipkan satu kata yang terlihat mirip ejaannya atau artinya, tetapi salah secara gramatikal/kolokasi. Kuasai 15 pasangan paling mematikan berikut:\n\n| Confused Pair | Exact Grammar Rule & Usage | Correct Mini-Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **make vs do** | `make` = menciptakan/menghasilkan (*make a decision, progress, mistake, profit, effort*); `do` = aktivitas/tugas (*do research, homework, business, an experiment, well*) | *They **did** extensive research before **making** a decision.* |\n| **like vs alike** | `like` = preposisi (sebelum Noun: `like + N`); `alike` = predikatif Adj/Adv (di AKHIR klausa, TIDAK diikuti Noun!) | *Mars, **like** Earth, has polar ice. / The two twins look **alike**.* |\n| **almost vs most** | `almost` = Adverb (\"hampir\": `almost all`, `almost never`, `almost 90%`); `most` = Determiner/Quantifier (`most people`, `most of the data`) | ***Almost** all samples reacted. / **Most** researchers agree.* |\n| **percent vs percentage** | `percent` = SELALU pakai angka (`25 percent`); `percentage` = TANPA angka (`a high percentage of`) | *Nearly **40 percent** of users... / A large **percentage** of users...* |\n| **rise vs raise** | `rise - rose - risen` = intransitif (naik sendiri, TANPA object); `raise - raised - raised` = transitif (menaikkan + OBJECT) | *Temperatures **rose** rapidly. / The central bank **raised** interest rates.* |\n| **lie vs lay** | `lie - lay - lain` = berbaring/terletak (TANPA object); `lay - laid - laid` = meletakkan/bertelur (+ OBJECT) | *The island **lies** off the coast. / Turtles **lay** eggs on the beach.* |\n| **affect vs effect** | `affect` = Verb (\"memengaruhi\"); `effect` = Noun (\"dampak/efek\": *have an effect on*) | *Noise **affects** concentration. / Noise has a negative **effect**.* |\n| **economic vs economical** | `economic` = berhubungan dengan ekonomi negara/bisnis; `economical` = hemat biaya/efisien | ***Economic** growth slowed. / Electric buses are more **economical**.* |\n| **beside vs besides** | `beside` = di samping (posisi fisik); `besides` = selain itu / di samping itu (`in addition to`) | *Sit **beside** the window. / **Besides** English, she speaks Japanese.* |\n| **no / not / none** | `no` + Noun (`no evidence`); `not` + Verb/Adj/Adv (`not valid`, `did not work`); `none` = Pronoun (`none of the data`) | *There is **no** proof. / It is **not** proven. / **None** of them passed.* |\n| **so vs such** | `so` + Adj/Adv (`so rapid that`); `such` + (a/an) + Adj + Noun (`such a rapid change that`) | *The storm was **so** severe... / It was **such a** severe storm...* |\n| **too vs enough** | `too` + Adj (berlebihan/negatif: *too heavy to lift*); Adj + `enough` (*strong enough*) vs `enough` + Noun (*enough time*) | *The signal is **too** weak. / The battery is **large enough**.* |\n| **say vs tell** | `say` (+ something / `say that`); `tell` + PERSON (+ something / `tell him that`) | *She **said** that it worked. / She **told** the dean that it worked.* |\n| **historic vs historical** | `historic` = sangat penting/bersejarah; `historical` = berkaitan dengan sejarah/masa lalu | *A **historic** peace treaty / **Historical** archives from 1800.* |\n| **number vs amount** | `number` = Countable Plural; `amount` = Uncountable | *A growing **number** of sensors / A small **amount** of oxygen.* |\n\n**Classic EPrT Trap:** Menulis `most all students` (harus `almost all`), `a small percent` (harus `percentage`), `alike other mammals` (harus `like other mammals`), `raise` tanpa objek, atau `make research` (harus `do/conduct research`).\n\n**WRONG:** **Alike** traditional silicon cells, modern perovskite panels **do** rapid progress and **rise** conversion efficiency significantly.\n**RIGHT:** **Like** traditional silicon cells, modern perovskite panels **make** rapid progress and **raise** conversion efficiency significantly.\n\n**WRONG:** A large **percent** of urban commuters reported that **most** every train arrived on time.\n**RIGHT:** A large **percentage** of urban commuters reported that **almost** every train arrived on time.\n\n**Flashcard Q:** Differentiate \"rise vs raise\", \"like vs alike\", and \"percent vs percentage\" in one crisp rule each.\n**Flashcard A:** Rise = goes up itself (no object) vs Raise = lifts something (+ object); Like + Noun vs Alike at clause end (no noun after); Percent needs a number (50 percent) vs Percentage uses no number (a high percentage).\n\n---",
